      "overview": "The lesson continues an important principle established in previous discussions: once a Qur'ānic text is expressed in a specific and unambiguous manner, its meaning cannot be altered through interpretation (bayān), specification (takhṣīṣ), or reinterpretation by another proof that comes later. Rather, the wording must be understood exactly as it was originally revealed unless there exists evidence establishing abrogation (naskh, النَّسْخ).\nThis distinction between modification and abrogation is essential for understanding the methodology of the Ḥanafī school. Many students mistakenly believe that all later evidence can clarify or restrict earlier evidence. The jurists explain that this is only possible when the original wording itself is capable of accepting clarification. A text that is already completely explicit leaves no room for additional explanation.\nThe scholars therefore summarize this principle by saying:\n«الْخَاصُّ لَا يَحْتَمِلُ الْبَيَانَ»\n\"A specific text does not admit further clarification.\"\nIn other words, the wording has already conveyed its intended meaning with complete precision. Nothing remains ambiguous that requires another proof to explain what was intended.",
