      "overview": "After completing the discussion of al-Khāṣ (الْخَاصّ), or the specific expression, the author begins the next major chapter in Uṣūl al-Fiqh by introducing al-ʿĀmm (الْعَامّ), the general expression. Although many classical primer texts simply state that the general expression is divided into two categories before discussing their legal rulings, the lesson explains that a proper understanding of al-ʿĀmm cannot be achieved without first mastering the concept of takhṣīṣ (التَّخْصِيص), or specification.\nThe instructor emphasizes that the objective of Uṣūl al-Fiqh is not merely to memorize definitions or classifications but to understand the legal principles (qawāʿid uṣūliyyah, قَوَاعِد أُصُولِيَّة) that govern the derivation of Islamic rulings. The legal value of a general expression depends upon whether it remains unrestricted or whether it has been specified. Consequently, before discussing the two categories of al-ʿĀmm, students must first understand the precise meaning of takhṣīṣ, how it differs from abrogation (naskh, النَّسْخ), and why the Ḥanafī jurists define it differently from many scholars of the majority (jumhūr, الْجُمْهُور). This methodological foundation is essential for appreciating the rulings that will later be assigned to each category of general expressions.",
