      "title": "Lesson 23 | The Definitiveness of General Texts in Hanafi Legal Theory",
      "overview": "In previous lessons, the concept of Takhṣīṣ (تَخْصِيص)—the specification of a general expression—was examined, along with the distinction between a general text that remains unrestricted and one that has been specified. The present lesson builds upon that foundation by addressing an important theoretical question: What is the legal force of a general expression before any evidence of specification appears?\nThe answer to this question determines whether jurists treat the wording of a general text as conclusive (qaṭʿī) or merely probable (ẓannī), and whether later evidence may restrict it with certainty or only with probability. This discussion became one of the most significant areas of disagreement between the Hanafi jurists and many scholars of the majority (Jumhūr – جُمْهُور).",
