      "title": "Lesson 24 | A Study of the Authority of General Texts: Example 1 (Theft)",
      "overview": "The present lesson examines one such issue: whether a thief remains financially liable for stolen property after the prescribed punishment for theft has been carried out when the stolen property no longer exists.\nThis discussion is presented within a broader chapter concerning the legal authority of unrestricted general texts (al-ʿĀmm العام). The author uses this juristic example to demonstrate an important principle of Ḥanafī legal theory: when a Qur'ānic text is expressed in unrestricted general language and no valid specification (takhṣīṣ تخصيص) exists, the wording remains definitive (qaṭʿī) in its application. Consequently, any ruling that appears to add to or restrict the meaning of such a text requires exceptionally strong evidence.\nThe discussion therefore extends beyond the law of theft itself. It illustrates the methodology of legal reasoning (uṣūl al-fiqh) and demonstrates how jurists derive practical rulings from the Qur'ān, the Sunnah, and rational legal principles.",
