      "portalId": "b0826acc-0ab8-4c72-b140-e5b76231d698",
      "title": "Lesson 27 | A Study of the Authority of General Texts: Example 4 (Breastfeeding)",
      "overview": "Islamic law is founded upon divine revelation and is implemented through precise legal principles developed by Muslim jurists. While the Qur'ān and the Sunnah establish legal rulings, the science of Uṣūl al-Fiqh (أُصُولُ الْفِقْهِ) provides the methodology by which these rulings are understood, interpreted, and applied. Throughout Islamic legal history, scholars have examined the wording of the Qur'ān and Sunnah with great care, determining whether a text is general or specific, unrestricted or restricted, and whether another text qualifies or limits its meaning.\nThe lesson summarized in this chapter presents one of the classical examples discussed in Uṣūl al-Fiqh: the legal consequences of breastfeeding (الرَّضَاعَةُ, al-raḍāʿah) and how jurists understood the Qur'ānic verse concerning foster relationships. Although the primary legal discussion concerns breastfeeding, the lesson first establishes the broader legal framework governing marriage in Islam. Understanding this framework is essential before examining the juristic disagreement regarding the amount of breastfeeding necessary to establish permanent marriage prohibitions.\nThis lesson therefore combines principles of Islamic family law with the methodology of legal interpretation, illustrating how the jurists reasoned from the Qur'ān, the Sunnah, and the principles of legal theory.",
