      "title": "Lesson 28 | Effect of Specification on General Texts",
      "overview": "The lesson examines an important question in uṣūl al-fiqh (أُصُولُ الْفِقْهِ), namely the legal status of a general expression after some of its individuals have been excluded through takhṣīṣ (تَخْصِيص), or specification. The discussion focuses particularly on the Ḥanafī treatment of al-ʿāmm al-makhsūṣ minhu al-baʿḍ (الْعَامُّ الْمَخْصُوصُ مِنْهُ الْبَعْضُ), meaning a general expression from which some of its individuals have been excluded. The central issue is whether the remaining individuals continue to fall under the original ruling with certainty, or whether the general text becomes probabilistic after specification.\nThe lesson builds upon an earlier distinction between an unrestricted general expression that has not undergone specification and a general expression from which some individuals have been excluded. The lecturer explains that specification does not ordinarily constitute abrogation. Rather, it clarifies that certain individuals were never intended to remain within the scope of the general ruling. This distinction becomes fundamental to understanding why the remaining individuals may continue to be governed by the general text while the certainty of that application is affected.\nThe discussion also introduces several technical concepts, including al-mukhaṣṣiṣ (الْمُخَصِّص), the evidence that causes specification, al-mukhaṣṣaṣ (الْمُخَصَّص), the general expression or individuals affected by specification, maʿlūm (مَعْلُوم), meaning known or identified, majhūl (مَجْهُول), meaning unknown, and iḥtimāl (احْتِمَال), meaning possibility or probability. Understanding these terms allows students to follow the more advanced discussion concerning the evidentiary force of general texts after specification.\nThe lesson further compares several positions within the Ḥanafī school. One position maintains that the general text loses its status as a definitive proof after specification. Another distinguishes between known and unknown instances of specification. A third, and ultimately emphasized position, maintains that the general text continues to apply to the remaining individuals, although its indication becomes ẓannī (ظَنِّي), probabilistic rather than definitive. This latter position permits further specification through evidences such as khabar al-wāḥid (خَبَرُ الْوَاحِد) and qiyās (قِيَاس).",
