      "portalId": "e20b0e38-6d2c-42e8-b09f-5b3fe88006e4",
      "title": "Lesson 32 | Al-Muṭlaq and Al-Muqayyad Part 4 (1st Example: Conditions of Ablution)",
      "overview": "The Verse of Purification and the Principle of the Unrestricted Command\nThe Islamic law governing ritual ablution (wuḍūʾ) — the act of purification required before the performance of formal prayer (ṣalāh) — rests primarily upon a single verse of the Qur'an, sūrat al-Māʾidah, verse six. This verse enumerates, in a compact and precise legal style, the four physical actions that constitute wuḍūʾ.\nيَا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا إِذَا قُمْتُمْ إِلَى الصَّلَاةِ فَاغْسِلُوا وُجُوهَكُمْ وَأَيْدِيَكُمْ إِلَى الْمَرَافِقِ وَامْسَحُوا بِرُءُوسِكُمْ وَأَرْجُلَكُمْ إِلَى الْكَعْبَيْنِ\nYā ayyuhā alladhīna āmanū idhā qumtum ilā al-ṣalāti faghsilū wujūhakum wa aydiyakum ilā al-marāfiqi wa'msaḥū bi-ruʾūsikum wa arjulakum ilā al-kaʿbayni\n\"O you who believe, when you rise up for prayer, wash your faces and your hands up to the elbows, wipe your heads, and [wash] your feet up to the ankles.\" (Qur'an 5:6)\nFrom this verse, the classical jurists (fuqahāʾ) derived four obligatory elements (arkān) of wuḍūʾ: washing the face (ghasl al-wajh), washing the two hands and forearms up to and including the elbows (ghasl al-yadayn ilā al-marāfiq), wiping the head (masḥ al-raʾs), and washing the two feet up to and including the ankles (ghasl al-rijlayn ilā al-kaʿbayn). The Ḥanafī school of legal theory (uṣūl al-fiqh) treats this verse as establishing wuḍūʾ in a manner that is unrestricted, or muṭlaq — that is, the four actions are commanded without any explicit condition regarding the presence of a conscious intention, a fixed order of performance, an uninterrupted continuity between the steps, or a verbal invocation of God's name at the outset.\nOver the following generations, however, jurists — most notably within the Shāfiʿī school — argued that four further conditions must be attached to this basic command before an act of washing can be legally recognised as valid wuḍūʾ: the condition of intention (niyyah), the condition of sequential order (tartīb), the condition of continuity (muwālāt), and the condition of the verbal mention of God's name (tasmiyah). This summary is devoted to a close, source-critical evaluation of the first two of these four proposed conditions.",
